WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:14.400
Okay, this is take number 14, tenuation of section 23.0 with Jack Schmidt.

00:14.400 --> 00:20.960
Okay, flight data file is CSM.

00:20.960 --> 00:27.040
Generally I have nothing but praise for the flight data file, both vehicles.

00:27.040 --> 00:36.640
There was very, very few things that one could have asked for to improve that data file for the 4.17 at any rate.

00:36.640 --> 00:41.160
One comment on the flight plane supplement.

00:41.160 --> 00:53.960
We had split pages for a number of the logging, medical and food logging, and that probably was a mistake.

00:53.960 --> 01:02.680
The idea was fairly good initially, but the pages we tended to only use that in the book as a whole, and it was a good place to keep them.

01:02.680 --> 01:10.960
And also the pages, if you had wanted them split, were too thin to maintain the split and they tended to fall out of the book.

01:10.960 --> 01:19.960
I would recommend either not splitting or having heavier paper, if you want them split.

01:23.960 --> 01:37.960
We had an extra cue card built for panel 229.

01:37.960 --> 01:47.960
I think it was an excellent card that summarized the circuit breaker functions, both on 229 and on panel eight.

01:47.960 --> 01:53.960
Because we had no systems and anomalies of any significance that would relate to that card.

01:53.960 --> 02:01.960
It was not used, but I would strongly recommend it's availability if only for training during the end or in Sims.

02:01.960 --> 02:09.960
It's a good quick review of what you lose or retain for those two panels.

02:17.960 --> 02:37.960
In the flight plan, I added some pen and ink cues along the margins for certain observational targets that I particularly wanted to look at.

02:37.960 --> 02:49.960
These were independent of any designated experiment and entered them as a function of time.

02:49.960 --> 02:53.960
That seemed to work very well for me.

02:53.960 --> 02:58.960
I think it had that kind of thing as an individual preference item.

02:58.960 --> 03:02.960
If you have experiment, it ought to be flight plan.

03:02.960 --> 03:10.960
If you're just planning to use your spare time for specific observational targets or types of observations,

03:10.960 --> 03:22.960
then I think the pen and ink is maybe the easiest way to go since it shouldn't concern any large number of people.

03:22.960 --> 03:40.960
We, Gordy Fullerton, fixed up the circular orbital cue card for me with a similar designation of craters as a function of time.

03:40.960 --> 03:49.960
I did not use that, not because it wasn't a good idea, but because of familiarity with the moon,

03:49.960 --> 03:53.960
which came very quickly after a couple orbits.

03:53.960 --> 04:04.960
You could recognize your position on the moon fairly easily as a function of each array of either the time and the red approximate time since sunset,

04:04.960 --> 04:08.960
or just because you could look out the window and tell where you were.

04:10.960 --> 04:19.960
In the limb, we, the same comments apply.

04:19.960 --> 04:26.960
I think all the flight data file items were excellent.

04:26.960 --> 04:35.960
I did not use the data card book to the extent that I'd always use on 15 and maybe on 16.

04:35.960 --> 04:55.960
We log most of our specific items such as alignment data and comparable kinds of things in the checklist at the point where they were collected rather than in the data.

04:55.960 --> 05:02.960
Book and had no, this did not seem to raise any difficulty at any time.

05:02.960 --> 05:12.960
The cuff checklist, we've talked about, I think, in the surface items.

05:12.960 --> 05:14.960
I thought the cuff checklist was excellent.

05:14.960 --> 05:16.960
I think we did the things.

05:16.960 --> 05:31.960
The right kind of photo maps in there that were useful for reference where we were around a given station.

05:31.960 --> 05:38.960
I don't think we used them as much as I had anticipated using them, mainly because navigation was no problem.

05:38.960 --> 05:56.960
In the points that we had selected preflight were excellent points for investigation and there was no need to try to decide on an alternative points to try to study in the vicinity of a given station.

05:56.960 --> 06:09.960
The list of items to be accomplished at each station are to really they were mind joggers to read at each station.

06:09.960 --> 06:24.960
We're not, again, not as used as much as I thought they would be initially, but I think that was mainly because we both at least I had become so familiar with the items that each station was in itself easy to recall.

06:24.960 --> 06:36.960
As a result of having created the checklist, so the checklist was turned out to be more of a learning item rather than a reference item for use on the surface.

06:36.960 --> 06:46.960
I wouldn't have done it any differently, however.

06:47.960 --> 06:57.960
I particularly want to compliment the Chuck Lewis on the timeline book.

06:57.960 --> 07:06.960
But I really can't say anything, but the timeline book was very, very well done and there had no problems with the timeline book at all.

07:06.960 --> 07:13.960
But that, of course, applies to every checklist that we had.

07:13.960 --> 07:20.960
There were just no procedural errors that I recall in any of the books that we used.

07:20.960 --> 07:25.960
Fortunately, we didn't have to use an malfunction book.

07:25.960 --> 07:43.960
I only, once did I pull out the systems data book to check on a systems problem and I, right now, I can't remember what that was.

07:43.960 --> 07:47.960
I didn't remember where it was, or I didn't use it once.

07:47.960 --> 07:59.960
The charts and maps, I thought in CSM that I would use the Orbiter Orbiter, what do they see?

07:59.960 --> 08:02.960
Orbiter charts, I guess that's what they're called.

08:02.960 --> 08:09.960
And I had an extra one put on, so I wouldn't interfere with the planned activities of the CMP.

08:09.960 --> 08:15.960
I did not use that very much.

08:15.960 --> 08:20.960
I eventually did some sketching on it, post DEI.

08:20.960 --> 08:28.960
I think there are about five specific points that I labeled as areas, A, B, C, D, maybe E.

08:28.960 --> 08:36.960
And these are referenced on my crew notebook, in my crew notebook for specific observations.

08:36.960 --> 08:40.960
But in general, I did not put that to as much use as I expected.

08:40.960 --> 08:45.960
One item is that that chart should have been identical to the CMP's chart.

08:45.960 --> 08:50.960
And there were a few pen and ink changes left off of it that caused some confusion at one point.

08:50.960 --> 09:00.960
And any exposure settings for certain photo targets.

09:00.960 --> 09:12.960
The CSM, the landmark maps that the LNP had added in the rear of that book.

09:12.960 --> 09:16.960
Again, we're not used.

09:16.960 --> 09:26.960
I really thought I would use those, but in the, you became reluctant, as you were observing a specific point,

09:26.960 --> 09:34.960
or area targets, they such as begin, or something like that, to take time out to sketch on the photo.

09:34.960 --> 09:39.960
I tended for the two or three minutes that observation was possible of a given area.

09:39.960 --> 09:49.960
I tended to look at the first opportunity to take notes in the notebook rather than trying to sketch on the photograph.

09:49.960 --> 10:04.960
And I suspect that this was because I tended to look for generalizations about the target that I was trying to observe, rather than checking out specific individual features.

10:04.960 --> 10:12.960
And they really did not seem to be any need to make notes about specific points on the pictures.

10:12.960 --> 10:15.960
And as a result, I did not use them.

10:15.960 --> 10:23.960
Again, though, I think having selected them and studied them pre-flight made it worth having them around.

10:23.960 --> 10:33.960
The necessity for flying them was probably less than the necessity for having reviewed them and studied them.

10:33.960 --> 10:44.960
I think I still, if I had to do it again, I still would want to have that kind of data available in the spacecraft, even though on this particular mission it was not used.

10:44.960 --> 10:56.960
I do think, though, that the CMP used his visual target maps considerably, and I did in a couple occasions to use some of the ones that he had again.

10:56.960 --> 11:07.960
But for the most part, that was post-tei, and I made some notes and sketches on some of those maps.

11:07.960 --> 11:14.960
And I think that just a function that there was a lot of time to look at the moon and make a sketch and then look back and fix it up.

11:14.960 --> 11:22.960
Post-tei, we're in orbit the time just to not exist.

11:22.960 --> 11:35.960
As Dick Gordon said a couple years ago, and once you start flying, clock is relentless.

11:35.960 --> 11:40.960
Okay, general flight planning.

11:40.960 --> 11:47.960
I was not very closely involved in the flight planning run, sort of carried the burden of that.

11:47.960 --> 11:54.960
For both Gene and myself, the flight plan was excellent.

11:54.960 --> 12:01.960
We had no problems with it at all that I'm aware of.

12:01.960 --> 12:08.960
Tommy Holloway and his people were to be complimented on that.

12:08.960 --> 12:27.960
A number of different requirements and experiments and general operational items that were required to be integrated one with the other was very, very high.

12:27.960 --> 12:38.960
was done in an extremely competent and usable way.

12:38.960 --> 12:52.960
And I don't can't think of anything that I would change in the way the flight plan was written.

12:52.960 --> 13:02.960
Okay, 23.5, preflight support.

13:02.960 --> 13:17.960
Preflight support was excellent in the flight data file area and one specific item that I had was once the flight plan was well established.

13:17.960 --> 13:27.960
Just about at the final stage, I had two or three texts I don't remember.

13:27.960 --> 13:42.960
Two or three briefing sessions were in the portions of the orbit flight plan that I was going to be during which I would be in the spacecraft and the CSM.

13:42.960 --> 13:56.960
We went over in detail the attitudes and maneuvers and the availability of the window availability so that I was able to plan in a very short amount of time with minimal effort on my part.

13:56.960 --> 14:09.960
The my own personal observations of the lunar surface and which went very well as far as planning was concerned.

14:09.960 --> 14:23.960
I appreciated that that was extra above and beyond the call of duty on the part of the flight planners and I appreciated their taking time out to do that for me.

14:23.960 --> 14:51.960
I think it was useful to have the I think it was a day long session where the flight controllers and the crew and the flight planners sat around in one room and went over those portions of the flight plan which were not normally simulated.

14:51.960 --> 15:04.960
It turned out that the flight planning had been so well done that I doubt if there were any specific items that came up that we need to do change or there was seem to be a lack of coordination on.

15:05.960 --> 15:25.960
This was in contrast to previous lights were I think flights were I think we were still learning all of us were learning on how to put together flight plans and a great requirements of seven Apollo 17 flight plan apparently reached the peak of perfection.

15:25.960 --> 15:36.960
I was a little bit disappointed in that briefing in that some of the people who would be eventually annually involved in the mission were not at the flight plan review.

15:36.960 --> 15:54.960
I think this was because of of conflicts with other programs which were the center was carrying out at the time.

15:55.960 --> 15:59.960
Okay 24.0 visual sightings.

16:00.960 --> 16:23.960
Well we've covered the countdown and launch I think pretty well in the previous sections.

16:24.960 --> 16:26.960
Also part flight earth orbit.

16:26.960 --> 16:47.960
I think the transcript would cover the visual sightings that I made with respect to weather and a few geographic observations and that same would go for the fairly extensive weather observations that I tried to make.

16:47.960 --> 16:54.960
Weather observations.

16:54.960 --> 17:15.960
I don't know whether I recorded yet that although we had light flashes just to about continuously during a whole flight when you when you were dark adapted.

17:15.960 --> 17:26.840
which I thought was a flash on the lunar surface during the first rev, that one period of time,

17:26.840 --> 17:32.040
when we had the blindfolds on for the off-met experiment, there were no visible flashes,

17:32.680 --> 17:38.040
although the next that evening, that night before I went to sleep, I noticed I was seeing the

17:38.040 --> 17:43.640
light flashes again, so it just seemed to be that one interval either side of that, that interval

17:43.720 --> 17:50.200
and either side of it were the light flash, and now I was not visible to myself, or to the other two

17:50.200 --> 18:05.160
crewmen. I think the transcript and my crew notebook would cover all of visual sightings that

18:06.520 --> 18:11.960
I can remember at this stage without going over to orbit by orbit and feature by feature with

18:11.960 --> 18:19.480
the photography, I think that has to come later. Entry we covered in the previous briefings

18:20.600 --> 18:27.480
and I think the same goes for landing and recovery, once just top of the second text.

18:28.360 --> 18:43.080
25.0 permission planning for the LNP, it's hard to think once again of anything that we didn't do

18:43.080 --> 18:49.480
right. I'm sure that might have been different had we had problems on the flight, but right the way

18:49.480 --> 18:55.080
the flight went, the total plan, the integration of the mission requirements into that plan.

18:58.280 --> 19:06.280
Although there were periods of some difficulty, pre-flight, particularly in the area of medical

19:06.280 --> 19:19.560
requirements, and in some last minute possible scientific requirements, particularly on the samples,

19:19.560 --> 19:26.760
everything seemed to get resolved satisfactorily in years I could tell, and I can't think of

19:26.760 --> 19:36.360
of anything that was not handled very well between the people who were leveling the requirements

19:36.360 --> 19:42.920
and the flight planners in particular, and occasionally when the crew had to be involved, that

19:42.920 --> 19:49.240
was handled almost entirely by the support crew of Bob Parker and the science area and

19:49.480 --> 20:09.720
the course of our training period, Ron, I guess the biggest single area that took time was

20:09.720 --> 20:16.360
one that Ron had to deal with and that was a lunar sounder. Most of our outset changes were

20:16.600 --> 20:25.560
taken care of prior to our training and we had a few minor suggestions that were taken care of

20:25.560 --> 20:35.720
early in the training cycle and we essentially had an up-to-date outset to train with

20:37.480 --> 20:42.440
and all other lunar surface gear was up-to-date for most of the 12-month training period.

20:42.600 --> 20:56.200
By the final 12 months of the training period, mission rules and techniques were fairly well defined

20:56.200 --> 21:02.200
very early by Phil Schaeffer and his crowd in the techniques area and the mission rules

21:02.200 --> 21:09.080
during Jerry Griffin and people working on that. No major changes and only the only changes were

21:09.400 --> 21:15.560
all I felt in the right direction and that they enhanced the probability of making a landing

21:15.560 --> 21:24.920
in a successful mission. They openly were in the area of opening up possibilities for work

21:24.920 --> 21:32.280
around so that a mission could be completed. We really never had to exercise any significant

21:32.280 --> 21:41.560
mission rules in a normal way. I think the one time that mission rule tended to

21:43.000 --> 21:50.200
let me finish this check. A mission rule tended to be fairly clearly a controlling factor was in

21:50.200 --> 21:59.560
the limitation on the work at station four or shorty crater where we were up against the walk

21:59.560 --> 22:09.160
back constraints and terminated that work after only 35 minutes were another 30 minutes there

22:09.160 --> 22:15.880
I think would have been extremely valuable although I hope that we got enough information that

22:16.200 --> 22:23.080
the phenomena exposed that that crater can be understood.

22:24.920 --> 22:36.760
26.0 mission control typically outstanding support for mission control.

22:37.160 --> 22:53.720
I think the this includes the number of extracurricular hours that the limb people and the e-cons

22:53.720 --> 23:03.400
for the CSM in particular put in with me on Saturdays and other times just generally talking over

23:03.400 --> 23:11.400
systems and techniques mission rules and that was a major factor in helping me understand and

23:11.400 --> 23:20.840
keep up to speed on the on those items. I think the help that they gave me and designing and

23:22.200 --> 23:28.280
in fact as I essentially did it did the design of the emergency cue cards that we did for

23:28.280 --> 23:38.280
Apollo 17 for the limb or was a major contribution although fortunately not we did not use them they

23:38.280 --> 23:46.600
still made it possible to understand very quickly systems problems and to solve those problems

23:47.480 --> 23:51.240
in the simulations and had we require them I think it would have gone very well.

23:51.240 --> 23:55.240
26.0

23:55.240 --> 24:06.440
Particularly one of the point out the help that Dick Thorson gave in organizing most of the limb sessions

24:09.240 --> 24:13.240
and even some of the joint CSM limb sessions that we had.

24:13.240 --> 24:21.240
26.0

24:21.240 --> 24:33.240
And he also Thorson also was a major organizer of the creation of and the updating of Bill's emergency cue cards.

24:43.240 --> 24:51.240
Even factors 27.0

24:51.240 --> 24:55.240
Let's go back. That's one other comment of 26.0.

24:56.240 --> 25:11.240
Both flight it's my understanding that some of the things I had hoped could be done during the flight were not possible because of

25:11.240 --> 25:16.240
real-time discussions in the mission control.

25:16.240 --> 25:32.240
Specifically one of those things was to have some summary of the thinking of the science background

25:32.240 --> 25:42.240
given to me while in flight and that would be the thinking based on the data that we had transmitted to them

25:42.240 --> 25:47.240
verbally and to since visually through the television camera.

25:47.240 --> 25:56.240
I had hoped that I would have the benefit of their thinking but apparently this was not possible to do.

25:56.240 --> 26:07.240
I would like to think that in the future we can look at ways of using the team approach to science investigations

26:07.240 --> 26:21.240
in space rather than depending solely on the observational capability and the interpreted capability of the men who are performing the job.

26:21.240 --> 26:32.240
There's no reason that I can see to not use all the brainpower that's available for any given task and part of that brainpower is on the ground.

26:37.240 --> 26:43.240
Okay 27.0

26:43.240 --> 26:48.240
Human factors, pre-flight.

26:48.240 --> 27:12.240
The broad of the discussions and organization from the crew point of view of the pre-flight health stabilization control program was born by the commander and the LNP just sort of went along with whatever was decided.

27:12.240 --> 27:27.240
I personally did not find any great difficulty in working out and adhering to the requirements of that program.

27:27.240 --> 27:49.240
I think medical care, all very limited requirement was good but a couple of sinus infections I had reacted just like they always had and we were able over a period of ten days or two weeks to get those cleared up.

27:49.240 --> 28:06.240
Time for exercise probably was less than it should have been although I was able to get a good workout just about every other day in addition to the workouts we got as a normal course of our EBA training.

28:06.240 --> 28:26.240
Eventually texts started scheduling putting on the schedule time in the late afternoon for exercise and that helped as a reminder and buffer to see that exercise was obtained.

28:26.240 --> 28:41.240
It is generally hard and at least in the lunar training program to get exercise periods in during the day and quite frequently the exercise was done in the Cape Gym at night.

28:42.240 --> 29:05.240
I think rest and sleep is an individual thing and I made a particular effort to always get as much as I possibly could and try never to get behind the park or on rest because my personal experiences that that is when I tend to get coals and resulting sinus infections.

29:05.240 --> 29:09.240
Medical briefing was good.

29:09.240 --> 29:17.240
The exams seemed to go very well in my estimation.

29:17.240 --> 29:23.240
They were as expeditious as possible under the circumstances.

29:24.240 --> 29:43.240
I think the operational medical personnel who carried out the exams are to be complemented in their efforts to see that the exam was as painless and as efficient as possible.

29:43.240 --> 29:53.240
This should also include the post flight exams on the Ticonderoga.

29:53.240 --> 30:04.240
Eating habits and amount of food consumption were normal except during those periods of time when we were on the in-flight food prior to launch.

30:04.240 --> 30:18.240
And those times that attended the in my case a decrease of appetite and certainly although the food was certainly palatable.

30:18.240 --> 30:27.240
My appetite did go down and it was not possible for me to eat the amount of food that was provided for me.

30:27.240 --> 30:51.240
This also applied to space work but in the case of the in-flight eating although I did not eat everything that was available to me in my food packages I apparently should need it to if I hadn't wanted to avoid losing weight.

30:51.240 --> 31:15.240
My appetite was down but also apparently for some reason I had a loss of weight and at this time of this recording my way to still down is not going up to pre-flight levels which may have been a little high by the way.

31:15.240 --> 31:37.240
Okay and 17.2 flight appetite food preference well I just discussed that a little bit and the appetite in-flight versus to each pre-flight was less again except for when we were testing the pre-flight food when I also had a low appetite.

31:38.240 --> 31:56.240
No notable differences in the taste of food I think the things I like pre-flight I liked in space and the things I didn't like I also didn't like I didn't notice any differences.

31:57.240 --> 32:13.240
Changing food preferences of flight progressed I tended to start to prefer to eat the wet packs and would eat those in preference to any of the other solid foods.

32:14.240 --> 32:25.240
I would strongly recommend my personal point of view at any rate that the wet packs be used in preference to the rehydrateable.

32:25.240 --> 32:36.240
I know I think that probably you get a different opinion from the other crewmen.

32:36.240 --> 33:04.240
After the first period of one and only period of difficulty with loose bowel movements I did cut out the potassium indicated foods and I can't say whether that had any effect or not but I did not have any other bowel movements and certainly no more loose ones before the end of the flight.

33:07.240 --> 33:20.240
The first bowel movement after flight on a tight time the roger was normal.

33:20.240 --> 33:35.240
The second was very loose, the third was normal and the fourth and fifth very loose.

33:35.240 --> 33:47.240
The size of food portions and the meal portions of course were pretty much constant in terms of availability.

33:48.240 --> 34:09.240
My appetite was very low the first day and gradually increased over the next two or three days and remained essentially the same after about the third day.

34:09.240 --> 34:14.240
I mentioned the most acceptable foods with the wet packs and the juices.

34:14.240 --> 34:23.240
The food cake I thought was good although it was possible to eat too much or to get to the point where you didn't want anymore.

34:23.240 --> 34:29.240
The chocolate was good.

34:29.240 --> 34:44.240
I think of the dry crackers or cookies or graham crackers were probably the most tasty.

34:44.240 --> 34:51.240
And peanut butter and jelly sandwiches were quite good.

34:51.240 --> 35:02.240
Deviations from the menu and the periods are all recorded in the flight transcript I'm sure.

35:02.240 --> 35:10.240
Food preparation and consumption.

35:10.240 --> 35:20.240
The rehydration went phenomenally and the normal gas was present.

35:20.240 --> 35:33.240
Food temperature tended to prefer the foods that were warm or hot and the hot water was quite adequate for warm foods.

35:33.240 --> 35:42.240
We actually missed the warm foods considerably in the limb where the hot water was not available.

35:42.240 --> 35:48.240
I did not notice an effect of water flavor or water was reasonably tasty.

35:48.240 --> 35:54.240
I never noticed a high chlorine taste of any kind.

35:54.240 --> 36:02.240
The gas content did make it a little bit uncomfortable to eat at times.

36:02.240 --> 36:07.240
Boom bowl packages worked pretty well although those were finally divided.

36:07.240 --> 36:10.240
I tended to cut off the other end of the package.

36:10.240 --> 36:16.240
The water insertion end and use them as a squeeze package.

36:16.240 --> 36:18.240
Spoons worked perfectly adequate.

36:18.240 --> 36:26.240
I attended not to use the fruit in the cans because of just the messiness of opening them.

36:26.240 --> 36:41.240
Those although I think the technique that Ron worked out was opening it in your inner ear mouth is a good one.

36:41.240 --> 36:57.240
It was only the fruits, canned fruits that I tended to avoid just because they were inconvenient to use.

36:57.240 --> 37:05.240
Food bars, near any EVAs, I think were good to have although I never ate more than half of one.

37:05.240 --> 37:17.240
It wasn't because it was untasteier just because of maybe a lack of interest in eating and using that time during the EVAs.

37:17.240 --> 37:24.240
I think after the EVA, before and after the EVAs and the limb ate very well.

37:24.240 --> 37:36.240
There were some things though we avoided and I guess in my case, after one having corn chowder once and it's simulating a major problem at although not a loose one.

37:36.240 --> 37:44.240
I did not eat corn chowder, I did not eat the cocoa, we could have tended to feel like I got a little more gas from cocoa.

37:44.240 --> 37:53.240
After taste I did not eat the shrimp.

37:53.240 --> 37:59.240
The seafood, the item shrimp and the lobster biscuit and these sort of things.

37:59.240 --> 38:06.240
Because of pre-flight I had noticed that they tended to have a long after taste.

38:06.240 --> 38:11.240
But otherwise I think all the other foods were certainly acceptable.

38:11.240 --> 38:32.240
I know many times I did not eat potato based foods because of just they were very filling.

38:33.240 --> 38:42.240
Food, waste, storage.

38:42.240 --> 38:46.240
